# Feature modules: one product area per `src/modules/<name>/`, enforced from day one

## Context and Problem Statement

The layers of ADR 0001 keep pure logic, HTTP, IO{% if kind == 'web' %} and the browser{% endif %} apart. Nothing keeps one product area from
reaching into another. As a project grows from one feature to several (chat, courses, newsletters), every domain
file can import every other, and an agent adding a feature has no boundary telling it where the feature ends.

ADR 0001 reserved `src/modules/*` for this but deferred the dependency-cruiser rule until the first module existed.
That left the boundary to the agent's memory.{% if kind == 'web' %} It also left a gap: `ui-not-server` listed the
server directories by name, so the UI could have imported a module and pulled server code into the browser bundle.{% endif %}

How should a product area be fenced so that the fence exists before the first one is written?

## Decision Drivers

* A boundary that must always hold is a gate, not a sentence (`docs/PLAYBOOK.md` §2).
* A feature can be reviewed, owned and removed as one folder.
* A directory added later is covered by the existing rules without anyone editing them.

## Considered Options

* Layers only: a feature is a set of files inside each layer (`src/domain/chat.ts`, `src/app/routes/chat.ts`, …).
* Feature folders inside each layer (`src/domain/chat/`, `src/infra/chat/`, …), each entered through an `index.ts`.
* Modules beside the layers: `src/modules/<name>/` holds one product area's server code and is entered through
  its `index.ts`.

## Decision Outcome

Chosen option: "Modules beside the layers". A product area's server code then lives in one folder that one set of
rules can fence, and the shared layers stay as they are. A small feature may still be a few files in the layers. A
product area with several files, its own tables or its own routes becomes a module.

The rules in `.dependency-cruiser.cjs` hold from day one, before the first module exists:

* `no-cross-module-internals`: a module reaches another module through that module's `index.ts` only.
* `modules-via-index-from-outside`: code outside `src/modules` enters a module through its `index.ts` only.
* `module-domain-pure`: a module's `domain.ts` (or `domain/`) imports `src/domain` and its own domain only.
* `module-not-infra`: a module imports `src/infra` for types only. `src/infra/server.ts` passes in its database,
  mailer, jobs and flags.
* `domain-independent`, `schemas-only-domain`{% if kind == 'web' %} and `ui-not-server`{% endif %} become default-deny:
  each forbids everything under `src/` outside its allowed directories, so a new directory, modules included, is
  covered without editing them.

This replaces the earlier plan in ADR 0001 to add the rule together with the first module; the rest of ADR 0001 stands.

### Consequences

* Good, because a product area's server code is one folder: one review, one CODEOWNERS line, one place to delete.
* Good, because no rule has to be remembered when the first module is added.{% if kind == 'web' %} The UI cannot
  import server code through a directory nobody listed.{% endif %}
* Bad, because wiring a module touches `src/app/app.ts` (its routes) and `src/infra/server.ts` (its dependencies).
* Bad, because server code now has two homes: the layers and the modules. `.claude/rules/modules.md` says which to
  use.

### Confirmation

`tests/arch/modules.test.ts` cruises a fixture with every forbidden edge next to the allowed ones. It fails when a
rule stops reporting or starts reporting an allowed edge. `just arch` (part of `just check`) applies the same rules to
`src/`. `.claude/rules/modules.md` gives the agent the shape of a module whenever it edits `src/modules/**`.

## Pros and Cons of the Options

### Layers only

* Good, because there is nothing new to learn.
* Bad, because a feature boundary exists only in file names; nothing stops `courses` from importing `chat` internals.

### Feature folders inside each layer

* Good, because the layer rules stay as they are.
* Bad, because one feature is spread over four or five folders, each needing its own index rule and review.

### Modules beside the layers

* Good, because one folder per product area, fenced by four rules that work before the folder exists.
* Bad, because a module's `index.ts` is one more public API to keep small.
